 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Degeneracy:** The mesh can become degenerate (e.g., vertices becoming colinear) during densification [S1]. This is handled by detecting degeneracy and re-meshing, although this rarely occurs in practice [S1].
-*   **Gradient Estimation:** The least-squares system for gradient estimation must be solved carefully [G]. If the one-ring neighborhood is degenerate (e.g., all neighbors lie on a line), the matrix $\mathbf{A}_v$ becomes singular [G]. A guard against this is required [G].
-*   **Continuity:** Standard barycentric interpolation is C0, leading to high-frequency artifacts and visible seams in fully opaque meshes [S1]. Clough-Tocher eliminates these by ensuring C1 continuity [S1].
+- **Degeneracy:** The mesh can become degenerate (e.g., vertices becoming collinear) during densification. This is handled by detecting degeneracy and re-meshing, although this rarely occurs in practice [S1].
+- **Gradient Discontinuity:** Standard barycentric interpolation produces a C0 feature field where the gradient is discontinuous across edges, causing high-frequency artifacts. The Clough–Tocher scheme eliminates this by ensuring C1 continuity [S1].
+- **Gradient Estimation:** The least-squares fit assumes a local planar approximation. If the neighborhood is sparse or noisy, the gradient estimate may be inaccurate, affecting the smoothness of the patch [S1].
